```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The BGP session between leaf_router_0_0 (AS 65200, 172.16.0.9/31) and
spine_router_0_1 (AS 65101, 172.16.0.8/31) is broken because
leaf_router_0_0's eth1 interface — the physical uplink toward
spine_router_0_1 — has been brought down at the Linux kernel level via
"ip link set eth1 down". The interface flags show <BROADCAST,MULTICAST>
with no UP or LOWER_UP bits, kernel state is DOWN, and there is no
172.16.0.8/31 connected route in the routing table. FRR's own interface
tracker agrees: "Interface eth1 is down / Link ups: 0, last: (never) /
Link downs: 1 at 12:18:56". Crucially, there is no "shutdown" statement
anywhere in leaf_router_0_0's FRR running config for eth1 — this is
purely an OS-level administrative shutdown, not an FRR-managed one.

As a result, FRR cannot establish a TCP connection to 172.16.0.8, so
the BGP session sits in Active state ("No path to specified Neighbor").
spine_router_0_1's side is fully healthy: eth1 is UP with carrier,
BGP config expects 172.16.0.9/remote-AS 65200, and all its other
sessions are established. Traffic between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 continues
because leaf_router_0_0 still has a working uplink (eth0) to
spine_router_0_0 and a valid BGP-learned route to 10.0.1.0/24 via
172.16.0.4 — the fabric is degraded but not broken.

Root cause: leaf_router_0_0 eth1 was administratively shut down at the
kernel level, severing the L2/L3 path to spine_router_0_1.

Corrective fix: on leaf_router_0_0, run "ip link set eth1 up". The
172.16.0.8/31 connected route will be reinstalled, FRR will detect the
link-up event, and the eBGP session to spine_router_0_1 will
re-establish within one connect-retry interval (~67 s per the current
timer).
=== END ===
```